## Section 1 — Mission

Codename: KILN.

Mission: Run a 70B-class Mixture-of-Experts model on a 2018 Dell
Latitude 7490 at 5 tok/s or higher with accuracy within 1% of FP16
on standard benchmarks. Then extend the same runtime up the hardware
ladder unchanged.

Non-goals: match Ollama on a dense model. Match llama.cpp on a single
backend. The dense path exists to prove correctness. The mission is
MoE, ternary, streaming, and per-layer heterogeneous dispatch.

## Section 2 — Prime Directives

1. The lowest-spec machine is the acceptance test.
2. One binary per platform, one model file, one API.
3. Per-layer dispatch, not per-machine modes.
4. Accuracy preserved. Every optimization is lossless or within
   documented tolerance on standard benchmarks.
5. Local by default. No network calls unless opted in.
6. Open-core. MIT or Apache-2.0.
7. Fail-safe. Backend failure falls back to CPU.
8. The masses come first.
9. No architecture change to accommodate one machine. If a flag is
   needed for KILN to work on a specific machine, that is a registry
   bug. Fix the registry.
10. Optional flags allowed. Per-machine builds forbidden.
11. Self-consistency is not correctness. Every kernel and every
    transformation gets a differential test against a reference.
12. The registry is the hot path. Every operation goes through it.

## Section 3 — Rule KILN-E36 — The Registry Is the Hot Path

The transformer must not call `proj_seq`, `dot_packed`, `q4k_matmul`,
or any kernel directly. Every operation goes through the dispatcher,
which consults the registry per operation per forward pass.

If a call site bypasses the registry, that is a bug, not an
optimization.

This rule exists so that ternary, MoE, diffusion, iGPU, NPU, and
every future backend can be added as registrations rather than as
branches in `dot_packed`. Without this rule, the project converges to
llama.cpp by accumulation: a mountain of `if weights.len() == X`
special cases, each added by someone who needed to ship.

The seam is the architecture.

## Section 4 — The Real Target

A 70B-A4B MoE model. 70B total parameters, 4B active per token.
Ternary quantized. In Q4_K that would be ~40 GB of weights. In
ternary, ~16 GB. Active weights per token: ~1 GB in ternary.

On Tier 0:
- Weights read per token: ~1 GB (active experts only)
- DDR4-2400 effective bandwidth: ~2.4 GB/s
- Raw floor: ~0.4 s per token
- With 85% expert cache hit rate: ~0.15 GB streamed per token
- Streaming cost: ~0.06 s per token
- Realistic target with fused kernels and 4 threads: 0.15-0.25 s/token
- **Target: 4-7 tok/s on Tier 0.**

Not 0.15 tok/s. Not 0.41 tok/s. 4-7 tok/s on a 70B MoE on a 2018
laptop. That is the mission.

Ollama cannot do this. llama.cpp cannot do this. MLX cannot do this.
Not because they are badly written, but because their design
assumption is "the model fits in RAM and runs on one compute unit."
That assumption fails on this machine.

## Section 5 — Why KILN Is Different

Every mainstream runtime — llama.cpp, Ollama, MLX, vLLM, exllama —
optimizes within one assumption: the model is resident in RAM and
the whole thing runs on one compute unit. Within that assumption
they are excellent. Outside it they cannot function.

KILN breaks that assumption in six ways. None of them are in any
shipped mainstream runtime.

1. **Per-layer heterogeneous dispatch.** Every layer, every operation
   can run on a different backend chosen per token from a DAG. The
   registry holds the constraints. The scheduler picks. Nothing on
   the market does this at the layer granularity on CPU.

2. **Predictive MoE expert streaming.** The model lives mostly on
   SSD. A per-layer prerouter predicts the next token's experts one
   step ahead, prefetches them, and keeps the hot set resident. This
   is what makes 70B fit in 16 GB. llama.cpp has one draft PR.
   Nothing shipped.

3. **Ternary inference in the shipping path.** 1.58-bit weights cut
   weight bytes 2.5x versus Q4_K. On a memory-bound machine, that is
   the entire speedup. BitNet.cpp exists as a research runtime.
   Nothing mainstream ships ternary as the default.

4. **Attention-guided diffusion generation for high-throughput
   regimes.** On CPU, diffusion LMs are compute-bound rather than
   memory-bound. The roofline paper gives a 64x arithmetic-intensity
   advantage at batch 64. Autoregressive for the first token,
   diffusion for the rest in regimes where latency matters less than
   throughput. Nothing mainstream does this.

5. **SSD as a first-class memory tier.** NVMe is not swap. It is a
   managed tier with its own eviction policy, prefetch hints, and
   layout. KVDRIVE and HERALD patterns. llama.cpp treats mmap as the
   OS's problem. We treat it as ours.

6. **Adaptive performance management.** Tier 0 machines throttle.
   The Dell 7490 reaches 90 C and settles at 30% sustained load. The
   runtime measures this continuously and adjusts the DAG. It does
   not assume a stable machine. Nothing mainstream does this.

The mission is those six. The dense path is the correctness
reference for the first of them.

