## Section 7 — The Six Pillars

Pillar 1 — Attention-Guided Diffusion Generation. Fork diffuse-cpp.
CLAD cluster-level parallel decoding. Inter-step KV cache reuse.
Attention-guided denoising. Eso-LMs for exact KV caching. TIDE for
MoE-dLLM interval-based expert refresh. HERALD for block-dLLM KV
offloading. ADLM for anchored diffusion quality. BitMamba-2 as SSM
alternative.

Pillar 2 — Ternary and Mixed-Precision Quantization. ATLAS TQ1.0
packing (built). T-SAR in-register LUT. Native BitNet b1.58. T-MAC
AVX2 LUT kernels (evaluated, do not retry — see Section 13).
MAGNET and Genkidama for validation. Trident for Rust ternary.
Camada for native ternary MoE. SmallThinker as target family. AYOT
for differentiable ternarization in the PTQ regime.

Pillar 3 — Predictive MoE Streaming with EDGE0-Style Prerouter.
Per-layer prerouters predicting next-layer routing one token ahead.
Expert-contiguous storage layout. Block-level prefetch with bounded
in-flight blocks. FlashMoE ML cache replacement (113 KB per-layer
model, 21% better than LRU). FIRM-MoE fine-grained decomposition.
ExpertFuse model-level merging. TIDE interval refresh. storage.llm
avg_gap_ema as reference.

Pillar 4 — SSD-Backed KV Cache with q8_0 Default. llama.cpp upstream
q8_0 KV cache as default. q4_0 for aggressive cases. HiFC pSLC
enterprise-only. HERALD for diffusion KV offloading. KVDRIVE
SSD-aware layout. RIS-Kernel for long-context sparse attention.
RaBitQCache as candidate.

Pillar 5 — Constraint-Aware Registry and DAG-Based Scheduler.
Capability Registry with conversion cost matrices, tensor residency
state, transfer latency, synchronization overhead, thermal state,
thermal history. Execution plan as a DAG. SlyOS as design reference.
Ghidorah ARCA for architecture-aware profiling.

Pillar 6 — Adaptive Performance Management. Continuous measurement
of CPU frequency, thermal state, RAM pressure, SSD throughput.
DAG adjusted in response. Degradation ladder: 100-80% no change;
80-60% disable speculative decoding; 60-40% drop KV precision;
40-25% smallest model; below 25% pause and notify. Preemptive
switch on predicted throttling. Restore within 60 s of recovery.

## Section 8 — Six-Layer Runtime Stack

Layer 6. User API. CLI, Ollama-compatible REST server, Python
bindings.

Layer 5. Automatic Mode Selector. Inspects model architecture and
hardware profile, produces a per-layer DAG plan.

Layer 4. Execution Plan. Nodes are computations annotated with
precision, placement, algorithm. Edges are data transfers with
measured cost.

Layer 3. Constraint-Aware Registry. Every backend declares supported
operations, precisions, memory model, available memory, bandwidth,
conversion costs, residency state, transfer latency, sync overhead,
thermal state.

Layer 2.5. Performance Monitor. Exposes live performance envelope to
the scheduler before every significant decision.

Layer 2. Memory Hierarchy Manager. Four storage tiers as one pool.
CPU L3, RAM, NVMe SSD, optional VRAM.

Layer 1. Backends. CPU (AVX2, AVX-512), CUDA, ROCm, Metal, Vulkan,
WebGPU, OpenVINO, CoreML, QNN, WASM.

## Section 9 — Implementation Roadmap

### Phase 1.5 — The Dispatch Seam. COMPLETE at commit a34625a.

The single unit of work that makes every subsequent addition a
registration rather than a fork.

Deliverables:
- `Operation` trait: matmul, attention, norm, embed, sample. Each
  with shapes, precisions, candidate backends.
- `Dispatcher` that takes an `Operation`, asks the registry for
  candidates, asks the scheduler to pick one, dispatches.
- `CapabilityRegistry` real implementation with at least one
  registered backend per operation. The backends are the current
  CPU scalar kernels.
- Replace every `self.proj_seq(...)` in `transformer.rs` with a call
  through the dispatcher.
- Differential test: chat on Qwen2.5-1.5B produces the same output
  as today, within 5% of the same speed. The only change is
  structural.

Gate 1.5: The output of `kiln chat` on Qwen2.5-1.5B is byte-identical
to the pre-seam build on the same prompt, seed, and max tokens. The
speed is unchanged within 5%. Every matmul goes through the
dispatcher. There is exactly one backend registered.

Anti-patterns:
- Do not add a second backend in Phase 1.5. The point is the seam.
- Do not optimize the dispatcher. It is called per layer per token.
  A trait object vtable is fine. Profile later.
- Do not special-case any operation. If a call bypasses the
  dispatcher, it is a bug.

### Phase 2 — Ternary in the Shipping Path. Weeks 3-4.

Deliverables:
- Register `cpu_ternary_tq1_0` as a backend. The kernel already
  exists (`kiln_tq1_0_matmul_scalar`, tested).
- Load a ternary GGUF. Convert Qwen2.5-1.5B to TQ1.0 with the
  existing packer, or download a native ternary model.
- Dispatcher picks `cpu_ternary_tq1_0` when the weight tensor's
  quantization is TQ1.0. No change to `transformer.rs`.
- Differential test: ternary output equals Q4_K output within
  documented perplexity tolerance on WikiText-2.

Gate 2: `kiln chat` on Qwen2.5-1.5B TQ1.0 is 2x or more faster than
on Q4_K, with WikiText-2 perplexity within 2% of the Q4_K baseline.

### Phase 3 — Fused and Multi-Threaded Kernels. Weeks 5-6.

Deliverables:
- Fused scalar matmul in C. Dequant and dot in one loop, no stack
  array. This is the fix for the scalar path's inefficiency.
- Multi-threaded projections across the row dimension via
  `std::thread::scope`. 4 cores on Tier 0.
- Fused attention. No materialized score vector.
- Fused embedding lookup. Dequant once, cache.
- The dispatcher's backend for matmul now points at the fused
  multithreaded kernel.

Gate 3: `hi there` reply on Qwen2.5-1.5B Q4_K in under 10 s, 0.9
tok/s or higher. This is the parity target with Ollama on the same
hardware.

### Phase 4 — Predictive MoE Streaming. Weeks 7-14.

The central KILN deliverable.

Deliverables:
